WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.939
Considering how singular Joaquin Phoenix's interpretation
of the Joker promises to be, it's probably

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:10.170
a good idea to know a little bit about the
character's long history to fully appreciate

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.880
what's different about his depiction.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.460
Here's everything you need to know before
watching 2019's Joker.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:23.160
Since his debut in 1940 's Batman #1, the
Joker has been Batman's most popular and enduring

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.080
arch-nemesis.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.260
As a result, he's appeared opposite every
big-screen version of the Caped Crusader's

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.660
adventures since 1966's Batman: The Movie.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.440
While the character's popularity has remained
consistent, the different cinematic Jokers

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.030
have taken a very different approach to the
tone of the character.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.809
The version played by Cesar Romero was a perfect
fit for the campy, relatively lighthearted

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:45.740
world imagined by the TV show.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.660
He was portrayed as more of a giggling, over
the top bank robber than anything else.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.580
When the Joker returned to theaters in Tim
Burton's 1989 Batman film with Jack Nicholson

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.500
under the makeup, he was played as something
a little more like a murderous performance

00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.000
artist.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.420
He was a hitman for the mob with a thing for
playing cards right from the beginning of

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.750
the film - and even killed Bruce Wayne's parents
in that version of Batman's origin story - but

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.440
his dip into the acid gave him a whole new
perspective.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:14.040
He not only took over Gotham City's crime
families for himself, he also plotted to kill

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.860
everyone in the city, dosing them with poisonous
gas as his masterpiece.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.820
Batman: The Animated Series hit television
screens in 1992, and the following year saw

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.880
the theatrical release of an animated movie,
Mask of the Phantasm.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.560
In both the show and the film, the Joker was
played by Mark Hamill, who might be best known

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.160
from his starring role as Luke Skywalker in
a little-known independent film called Star

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:36.660
Wars.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:41.660
Hamill's version of the character was beloved
by fans, and he kept the role in various animated

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:46.500
shows and video games through today, despite
flirting with retirement from the part after

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.320
Batman: Arkham City.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.100
2008 brought us The Dark Knight, and Heath
Ledger's unforgettable, Academy Award-winning

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:52.700
performance.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.380
This time, unlike Burton and Nicholson's take,
the Joker had no origin story, and even told

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.640
conflicting versions of his history to different
characters throughout the film.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.280
"My father was a drinker."

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.819
He billed himself as an "agent of chaos,"
with intricate, clockwork plans that were

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:16.900
designed to destroy any sense of morality
that Batman had returned to his city.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.340
With the launch of DC's movie universe, we
got another version of the Joker in Suicide

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:25.799
Squad, which saw Jared Leto apparently going
full method actor in order to embrace the

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.300
Joker's sinister personality.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:32.820
In this take, he was a heavily tattooed, unpredictable
crime boss with a gift for manipulating people

00:02:32.829 --> 00:02:37.219
like his sidekick, Harley Quinn, a character
who had originally appeared in the animated

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.520
series and rose to incredible popularity in
the decades since.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.660
With that many different portrayals - not
to mention all of the different interpretations

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:48.790
of the Joker that have appeared in comic books
over the past 80 years - the idea that any

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.560
single actor could be the only "true" Joker
is as insane as the character himself.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.420
Even with that in mind, though, Joaquin Phoenix's
portrayal in the Joker's first-ever solo film

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.640
promises to be a pretty big departure from
what we've seen before.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.080
One of the first questions fans were asking
when Warner Bros. announced that Phoenix had

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.360
been cast as the Joker was: what happened
to Jared Leto?

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.920
At one point there were no fewer than six
Joker-related films in development with Leto

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.260
still attached for some of them.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.460
Were the filmmakers just replacing Leto and
being coy about it?

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.160
The answer: Leto's Joker and Phoenix's Joker
aren't the same guy.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.430
The Joker of 2019 will not be part of the
DC's movie universe - it's a standalone film

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:34.010
and has nothing to do with Suicide Squad,
2017's Justice League, 2021's The Batman,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.260
or any other DC films.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.200
That actually may have something to do with
why Phoenix accepted the lead in Joker.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.900
He came close to playing Doctor Strange, turned
down the role of Superman's nemesis Lex Luthor,

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.519
and auditioned for the part of the Red Skull
in 2011's Captain America: The First Avenger,

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.240
but never wound up appearing in any of those
movies.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.700
The major difference between those roles and
the role of Joker is that signing up for Lex

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.960
or Strange would have obligated Phoenix to
appear in other movies.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.920
With the new Joker film being billed as a
stand-alone, character-driven drama rather

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:06.909
than a franchise blockbuster, Phoenix can
play the character without signing on for

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:09.380
a sprawling series of superhero movies.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.660
One of the things that makes this Joker distinct
from most other versions is that we're actually

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.500
learning the name he was born with - Arthur
Fleck.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.380
It isn't the first time a writer has given
the Clown Prince of Crime a proper first and

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:22.360
last name.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.700
In the Gotham TV series, the Joker is Jeremiah
Valeska.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:31.330
In Tim Burton's Batman, Joker is known as
Jack Napier, a suitably comic booky pun on

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.940
"jackanapes," another word for a fool.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.940
"You are my number one guy."

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.520
In most other versions, including the comics
canon and the award-winning version in The

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.760
Dark Knight, however, the Joker goes unnamed.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:50.139
Even in Batman: The Killing Joke - a 1988
graphic novel by Alan Moore and Brian Bolland

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:54.199
that includes a Joker origin story - in all
of the flashbacks to Joker's past we never

00:04:54.199 --> 00:04:56.180
learn so much as his first name.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:01.140
"You are my number one guy."

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.440
We all know Joaquin Phoenix will be Joker's
lead, though some fans theorize we'll eventually

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.569
learn that this particular clown isn't the
"real" Joker.

00:05:11.569 --> 00:05:15.140
He won't be on the screen alone, though, and
there are plenty of other interesting names

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.260
as part of the cast.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.340
Appearing as Thomas Wayne is Brett Cullen,
a distinguished character actor with a long

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:22.379
career in Hollywood beginning in the '80s.

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:27.129
What's interesting about his appearance here
is that Joker will be Cullen's second Batman-related

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:30.580
film - he plays a congressman in 2012's Dark
Knight Rises.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.880
Frances Conroy plays Fleck's ailing mother
Penny.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:38.060
She's perhaps best remembered for her role
as Ruth Fisher in HBO's Six Feet Under, but

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.660
she also has a surprising Bat-related film
connection: she played the small but crucial

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.490
part of Ophelia in 2004's legendarily bad
Catwoman.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:51.040
We also see Zazie Beetz briefly in the Joker
trailer.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:56.600
Fans will likely remember Beetz's performance
as Domino in 2018's Deadpool 2, but in Joker

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.720
she plays Sophie Dumond - a single mother
and Fleck's love interest.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.180
One of the most interesting casting choices
is that of Robert De Niro as talk show host

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.120
Murray Franklin.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:11.180
Fans and critics are speculating Joker will
in many ways be strongly influenced by Martin

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.440
Scorsese's 1982 darkly satirical The King
of Comedy.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:19.840
De Niro plays a struggling, mentally ill comic
in that film, just as Phoenix plays one in

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:20.340
Joker.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:24.979
The young Bruce Wayne we see Fleck forcing
to smile with his fingers in the trailer is

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:30.009
played by Dante Pereira-Olson, and it won't
be the first time he's worked with Phoenix.

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:33.390
Pereira-Olson was in You Were Never Really
Here, where he played the younger version

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:37.340
of Phoenix's character - another mentally
ill man living with his elderly mother.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.900
From what we've seen in the trailer, Joker
doesn't seem like an adaptation of any single

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:42.860
comic book story.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.360
However, there are glimmers of inspiration
from specific comics, and at least one aspect

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.200
of Joker is similar to one of the most influential
Joker stories of all time.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:56.560
Written by Alan Moore - famous for Watchmen,
V for Vendetta, and other groundbreaking comics

00:06:56.569 --> 00:07:01.770
of the '80s - and drawn by Brian Bolland,
The Killing Joke is memorable for many reasons,

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.380
one of which is the origin story given to
the Joker.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.780
We never learn his name, but the younger Joker
is an aspiring stand-up comic who can't get

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:09.280
a break.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.539
No one laughs at his jokes, he isn't making
any money, and his young wife has a baby on

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:14.040
the way.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.780
Desperate, he agrees to go on a heist with
some crooks and falls into a vat of chemicals

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.460
that turn him into the villain we all know.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.780
Like this unnamed young Joker, Arthur Fleck
seems to be a failed comic, but that may be

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:25.960
where the similarities end.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:30.500
Rather than the classic comics origin of a
fall into a vat of chemicals, Fleck seems

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:35.129
to be a man driven to and beyond the brink
by the madness of society, using makeup to

00:07:35.129 --> 00:07:38.200
adopt the Joker's persona much like Ledger's
version did.

00:07:38.349 --> 00:07:41.699
The other major influence on Joker doesn't
come from the comics page.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.900
Instead, the visuals we see in the trailer
see heavily influenced by The King of Comedy.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:51.159
While it's not as well known as hits like
Taxi Driver or Goodfellas, the 1983 collaboration

00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:55.620
between Martin Scorsese and Robert De Niro
became a cult classic for its portrayal of

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:59.639
a struggling comedian who develops a disturbing
fixation on a Johnny Carson-esque talk show

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:00.300
host.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.520
"Mom!”

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:04.340
"Take it easy lower it."

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.260
"I'm not gonna lower it I have to do this
now!"

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:12.499
De Niro stars as failing comic Rupert Pupkin,
while Jerry Lewis plays the very successful

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:13.420
Jerry Langford.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:18.340
While he doesn't dress up like a supervillain,
like Fleck, Pupkin is portrayed as being mentally

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:22.520
ill and the film ends with some ambiguity
about whether or not the viewer is experiencing

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.100
actual events or Pupkin's delusions.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.900
Joker is even set in the early '80s, just
like The King of Comedy.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:32.050
The influence isn't just evident from the
trailer, however.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:36.300
Scorsese himself was originally set to be
executive producer for Joker, but left the

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:37.960
project due to scheduling issues.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:42.060
Regardless, the final project seems to have
his fingerprints all over it.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:46.440
Moviegoers expecting to see the Dark Knight
showing up in Joker to face off with his nemesis

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.020
are bound to be disappointed, but the film
isn't entirely free of Batman.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:54.160
We're just seeing him long before he decides
to dress up as a bat and spend his evenings

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:55.020
fighting criminals.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.300
Given that we're getting a young Bruce Wayne
and a prominent role for his dad, it's possible

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:04.000
that Joker might include the murder that kicks
off Batman's own origin story, but whether

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.220
or not that will actually happen remains to
be seen.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:10.640
It's clear, however, that no matter what happens
to Thomas and Martha, their son will spend

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:14.200
this film a long way from donning his familiar
comic book costume.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.980
Joker is no superhero movie.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:22.080
Instead, the cast and director Todd Phillips
have spoken of the movie as a far more intimate

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:26.780
character piece and a study of mental illness
rather than an action film about a killer

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:30.750
clown squirting acid at cops out of flowers
on his lapel, surrounded by henchmen.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:35.490
It will, however, take place in Batman's fictional
hometown of Gotham City, complete with some

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:39.700
familiar landmarks, although it seems to be
a more realistic one than what we might be

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:40.260
used to.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:45.600
The trailer has a few shots outside and inside
Arkham State Hospital, which is a much more

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.680
real-world version of Arkham Asylum than we've
seen in other Batman media.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:54.820
In the comics, cartoons, and video games,
Arkham often appears as an impossible nightmare

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:58.780
that looks like a gothic mansion that you
would expect to show up in an old Dracula

00:09:58.780 --> 00:09:59.280
movie.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.520
The Arkham of Joker looks more like what you'd
find in the real world.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:07.740
It's a "State Hospital" rather than an "Asylum,"
a term that fell out of favor decades ago.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:11.820
Fleck appears to be walking there across a
city street, whereas in most portrayals it's

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:14.920
out in the middle of the wilderness past imposing
wrought iron gates.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:20.100
Phillips wants us to see Fleck in the same
cold, sterile, uncaring bureaucracy the mentally

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:24.810
ill have to navigate in our own world, rather
than a place that looks like an immense haunted

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.820
house with prison cells designed for crocodile
men.

